<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oliver Tambo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1993">1993</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oliver Tambo was a South African anti-apartheid politician and activist who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> from 1967 to 1991. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a> and Walter Sisulu he helped found the ANC Youth League in 1944, and became the ANC's secretary-general in 1955. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharpeville-massacre">Sharpeville massacre</a> of 1960 the ANC sent him abroad to rally international opposition to apartheid; based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, he became acting president on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-luthuli">Albert Luthuli</a>'s death and was directly responsible for organising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> units. He returned in December 1990 after more than thirty years in exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oliver-tambo.jpg" alt="Oliver Tambo" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rob Croes for Anefo · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oliver_Tambo_(1981).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
